Name: matchAll

Type: function

Syntax: matchAll(<string>, <regularExpression>)

Summary:
<return|Returns> an <array> of every match of a <regular expression> in
the specified <string>.

Introduced: 0.9.16

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop, server

Example:
local tMatches
put matchAll("a1 b22 c333", "[a-z]([0-9]+)") into tMatches
-- tMatches[1][0] is "a1",  tMatches[1][1] is "1"
-- tMatches[2][0] is "b22", tMatches[2][1] is "22"
-- tMatches[3][0] is "c333", tMatches[3][1] is "333"

Example:
-- Count the words in a string
put the number of elements in matchAll(tText, "\w+") into tWordCount

Example:
-- Named groups are also available by name
local tPairs, tPair
put matchAll("width=80;height=24", "(?<key>\w+)=(?<value>\w+)") into tPairs
repeat for each element tPair in tPairs
   put tPair["key"] && "is" && tPair["value"] & return after tReport
end repeat

Parameters:
string (string):
The text to search.

regularExpression:
Any expression that evaluates to a regular expression.

Returns (array):
The <matchAll> <function> returns a numerically keyed <array> with one
element for each match, in the order the matches occur in the <string>.
Each element is itself an <array>: key 0 holds the text of the whole
match, key 1 the text matched by the first parenthesized group, key 2
the second, and so on. If nothing matches, the <function> returns empty.

Description:
Use the <matchAll> <function> to find every place a pattern occurs in a
<string>, rather than only the first as <matchText> and <matchChunk> do.

The search for each match begins where the previous match ended, so
matches do not overlap. The text before that point is still taken into
account: "^" matches only at the real start of the <string> (or of a
line, with the "(?m)" option), and "\b" and lookbehind assertions see
the characters that precede the match.

Every parenthesized group of the <regularExpression> has a key in every
match. A group that took no part in a particular match, such as an
optional group, is empty.

A named group, written "(?<name>...)", is stored under its name as well
as under its number.

A pattern that can match no characters, such as "a*", produces an empty
match at each position where nothing longer matches. After an empty
match the search moves on by one character, so the <function> always
finishes.

The <string> and <regularExpression> are always <case-sensitive>,
regardless of the setting of the <caseSensitive> <property>. (If you
need to make a <case-insensitive> comparison, use "(?i)" at the start of
the <regularExpression> to make the match case-insensitive.)

By default a <regular expression> works on text one UTF-16 code unit at
a time and treats only ASCII characters as letters and digits. Two
switches, written at the very start of the <regularExpression>, change
this:

- "(*UTF)" makes "." and character classes treat a character outside the
  Basic Multilingual Plane, such as an emoji, as one character rather
  than two, and allows Unicode property classes such as "\p{Greek}".
- "(*UCP)" makes "\w", "\d", "\s", "\b" and the "(?i)" option follow
  Unicode rules, so that accented and non-Latin letters count as
  letters.

They can be combined, and must come before anything else, including
"(?i)": for example "(*UTF)(*UCP)(?i)\w+".

If the <regularExpression> is not valid, or matching it needs so many
steps that it is abandoned (which can happen with nested repeats such as
"(a+)+$"), the <function> throws an error.

>*Note:* At most 49 parenthesized groups are returned for each match.

>*Tip:* HyperXTalk implements <regular expression|regular expressions>
> compatible with the PCRE library. For detailed information about
> <regular expression> elements you can use with this
> <function(control structure)>, see the PCRE pattern documentation at
> http://www.pcre.org/original/doc/html/pcrepattern.html.

References: filter (command), matchChunk (function),
matchText (function), replaceText (function), array (glossary),
regular expression (glossary), case-sensitive (glossary),
case-insensitive (glossary), return (glossary), function (glossary),
string (keyword), caseSensitive (property)
